=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
The affected subsystem is the BGP daemon (`bgpd`) on **router1**. The process is simply not running, even though it is explicitly enabled (`bgpd=yes` in `/etc/frr/daemons`) and carries a syntactically valid configuration in `/etc/frr/frr.conf` (AS 1, neighbor 193.10.11.2 remote-as 2, advertising 195.11.14.0/24).

Without bgpd running on router1, no BGP TCP session can be established on the 193.10.11.0/24 peering link. Router2's bgpd is healthy and actively trying to reach its peer — it shows neighbor 193.10.11.1 stuck in `Active` state, meaning it is repeatedly attempting the TCP handshake to port 179 and getting no answer. As a result, neither router has exchanged prefixes: router1's RIB contains only its two directly connected subnets (193.10.11.0/24 and 195.11.14.0/24) and has no route to pc2's 200.1.1.0/24. When pc1 sends traffic to 200.1.1.2, router1 immediately returns ICMP "Destination Net Unreachable" — exactly the failure pc1's user is seeing.

The root cause is that the bgpd process on router1 crashed or was killed and was not restarted, leaving the FRR stack running only zebra/staticd.

**The team's instinct to restart router2 is wrong** — router2's bgpd is completely healthy. The fix is to restart FRR on **router1** (`service frr restart` or `systemctl restart frr`). This will spawn bgpd, load frr.conf, bring up the eBGP session to router2, exchange the two /24 prefixes, and restore full pc1 ↔ pc2 connectivity within seconds.
=== END ===